TimedEvent
==========

A small C# wrapper around System.Threading.Timer. It raises an event at a chosen interval and passes an object to the handler. Version 1.0 was written August 25, 2009.

Use it
------
Include TimedEvent.cs in your project. The class is in the com.nk.lib namespace and has internal visibility, so this example assumes the same assembly:

    var timer = new com.nk.lib.TimedEvent(1000, 1000, "example");
    timer.eventTrigger += (sender, args) => {
        Console.WriteLine(sender);
    };

Keep the object alive for as long as you need it. Stop it with:

    timer.disposeTimer();

The first two constructor arguments are the initial delay and repeat interval in milliseconds. The third is delivered to the handler as sender. A positive initial delay gives the caller time to subscribe after construction.

Limits
------
Callbacks run on a thread-pool thread, not a UI thread. Slow callbacks can overlap. The wrapper does not marshal UI updates, catch callback exceptions, or implement IDisposable. Disposing the timer does not mean an already queued callback has finished.

There is no project file or automated test suite here. Modern .NET compatibility was not tested in this documentation pass. See LICENSE.TXT for licensing.
